#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162712 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162043 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b36c482-75a0-5413-8e3e-4a72e3ddb26a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96727cbd-6aa9-551e-9adf-2bc05660497d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161565} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa2d132-67e0-564d-8c39-da8ce485f972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161566} {:answer/id #uuid "36834f60-b8ee-5755-aab5-e51620ae6b52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161567} {:answer/id #uuid "42f91bbb-1b10-5f2b-817a-8346ff722cd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161568} {:answer/id #uuid "4516e4c5-ba12-5d8f-a13c-6ad031f8ca90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161569}] :answer-field/id #uuid "53075006-3bde-5335-a07e-7b2f11fef0a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161564}] :question/id #uuid "0acab5c5-194e-580b-922b-276032b93c12" :question/math-academy-id "q-97365" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, V = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A = UΣV^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A$, which of the following could be the $3$rd column of the matrix $U$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162043 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b36c482-75a0-5413-8e3e-4a72e3ddb26a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65acaa42-33cd-5757-90f9-4d51036d283f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371424} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e77905-5ec8-56d5-8779-68fa5b781883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371425} {:answer/id #uuid "97daab37-f8ae-5701-b85b-b2e30894a4f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371426} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e0b9ef-caf7-5749-a8a6-77538e234c6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371427} {:answer/id #uuid "a5fd5a22-26d6-55d8-a7db-98cfc7d4ab6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97daab37-f8ae-5701-b85b-b2e30894a4f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371426} :answer-field/id #uuid "89ed90b3-a22c-5487-9e5f-dfdcd163748b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd52405b-2747-5b7b-9ac8-728557a18405" :question/math-academy-id "q-98769" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the $3$rd column of the matrix $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st and $2$nd columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\end{aligned}\\,‖{u}_{3}‖=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the nonzero normalized solution \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}]}
